# An Elder's Greetings!
Tamil title: மூத்தவர் வாழ்த்து!

`Vazhga`—"may you live and flourish"—is not merely a Tamil word; it is a Tamil quality. To offer a heartfelt wish that another person's life may prosper and that they may be happy, because this adorns not only that person but society as well, is a Tamil tradition. Anyone with a heart can feel the music and tender emotion within that word.

Each person's life is founded upon their own effort. Even children know this lesson. Yet if anyone's effort is to proceed without obstruction and shine in success, the actions of countless others like them must lend support and confer excellence.

The quality of the farmer's hand determines the excellence of the plough. Yet must not the nature of the soil also be suitable? However skilfully one may labour, can one plough a black stone outcrop, turn it into a field fit for cultivation, and reap a benefit?

Likewise, even when an individual's effort is excellent, society must be appropriately constituted if that effort is to yield its proper fruit.

For society to be so constituted, others too must engage in worthy action and reap its benefit. That is why the practice of greeting another with `Vazhga` arose. For one person to live, must not others live too? Must there not be a plant if one is to see a flower?

The musical sound `Vazhga` arose upon the foundation of this splendid quality.

The Tamil heart is enchanted when it sees beauty, tormented when it sees sorrow, and leaps wherever it encounters joy.

For long ages, the Tamil mind has been cultivated to praise noble action and rise in inspiration at the sight of courage.

To understand how elevated this quality is, we need not look only at the Tamils' adoption of the practice of blessing others. We can understand it well from the joy with which they blessed nature itself, and not merely people.

They hailed the sun, moon, great rain, flower, waterfall, dancing peacock, music-learning cuckoo, and caressing parakeet. They praised courage, generosity, and learning. The Tamils made it their tradition to bless everything that constituted wealth in life.

In those ancient days when great poets stood upon the path of virtue and shone as ornaments of Tamil Nadu, the sound of benediction spread and gave fragrance everywhere.

Then alien and empty sounds arose to such an extent that one could worry that Tamil Nadu had lost even this musical cry of `Vazhga`, and fear that Tamil tradition itself might be abandoned.

Yet, as forest becomes cultivated field, as light returns to a darkened sky, and as buried gold and gems are found by those who seek them, that beautiful and delightful sound expressive of Tamil tradition—the music of `Vazhga, vazhga!`—which had lain dimmed in a condition that made us fear it might die, has risen again in Tamil Nadu.

The new day of Pongal has arrived as a sacred day on which every home drinks and delights in that honeyed word, `Vazhga`. A radiant home, a heart brimming with joy, and a nature that praises and rejoices: these are the beautiful ornaments of this sacred day.

Today, as though displaying every variety of what may be worn as ornament, applied to the body, eaten, and worn as clothing, we see Tamils move about in a resplendent aspect, overflowing with happiness.

When we hear a lion raise its voice and roar even while confined within a cage, its astonishing courage draws our hearts. But if a donkey, fitted with a silken saddle and bells upon its feet, proceeds unhindered in procession along the highway and supplies its own "music," who could listen and endure it?

Today the Tamil is a caged lion. The cry that arises in this condition therefore cannot help but astonish those who hear it.

When people say mockingly, "Did you hear? The new day of Pongal is apparently a sacred festival of the Tamils!" do not think they are merely speaking words of ridicule. The meaning is that they combine what remains of their swagger with a fearful thought and cast it out to test us: "They have awakened; they have gained understanding; therefore they will certainly win liberation."

When they say, "Apparently `Vazhga` is their mode of speech, their tradition, their character," understand that fear makes them spit out that word because they have realized that the foundations of the domination they guarded behind a thousand and eight fortifications have cracked and begun to shake.

It is two in the morning. In a state both embraced and not embraced, the beautiful woman lies asleep with the treasure she bore placed beside her. Milk and moonlight mingle harmoniously upon that fresh golden face and give it radiance. The child is asleep, yet laughs merrily. Have you heard it? Have you seen that sight?

Can either the *yazh* or the flute produce a sound sweeter than that laughter?

Yet to the ear of the thief who has entered to steal, that sound is a scorpion, not honey.

Likewise, the sound `Vazhga` is music to us. To those who have cast us—who once lived musically—into distress and now dominate us, that very word becomes the roar of a lion. That too is necessary!

The Tamils possess the tradition that everyone must live, and that our own life rests upon it. All that is needed for them to lead a good life exists here without deficiency; anyone but a person of deficient understanding knows this.

The maiden called Earth comes dancing like a beautiful woman, singing of abundance and laughing as she asks, "When I am here, why utter the word 'poverty'?" The daughter of the mountain, the waterfall, says, "Behold my appearance!" The trees, her foster daughters, sing in harmony, "Yes, yes!" The sea-maiden says that she possesses countless "pearls." Yet if one wants a sweet kiss, it is not dried ginger or pepper that can be obtained easily. One needs courage that fears neither whirlpool nor shark. "I shall embrace with my wave-like arms those who display that courage and shower them with kisses," she says every day.

Every resource needed to give the assurance that labour enables one to live is present here.

Because all this fine abundance is nature's beauty, our Tamils stood and blessed it.

We still offer our blessings. Though others have arranged for so much darkness to surround us, we have understood well who we are. We offer our blessings. But it is not proper to stop there. Therefore, never forgetting that giving will triumph, let us join the noble and sweet endeavour that has arisen without deviation from the declaration that Tamils will overcome sorrow and live. Let us break our fetters and behold the light of the lamp of courage in Tamil Nadu. Come!

May every one of your homes and hearts assume a festive aspect so that Tamil warriors may gain this awakening, and so that the women of the martial lineage—dearer than my own life, great heroes—may mingle joyfully with their children and utter it. The flower that is my younger brother, the man of *Murasoli*, offers this: "Though beauty is everywhere—upon the lips, at the waist, and in the glance one gives—add my greeting to all that beauty and joy and rejoice."

I beat my younger brother's drum for a little while and delight in it. Have you not always possessed my greetings?

May the Tamils live and attain every prosperity!

May the Tamils live so that the world may live!

May Tamil virtue live so that the earth may flourish!

May the Tamil language live and overflow with sweetness!

*Murasoli Pongal Malar*, 14 January 1961.

---NOTES---
`வாழ்க` is retained once as `Vazhga` and immediately glossed because the essay treats its sound, grammar, and cultural resonance as its subject. The caged lion and decorated donkey form an explicit political contrast. The final reference to “my younger brother, the man of Murasoli” personifies or affectionately identifies the journal and its editor within the elder's greeting.
